  "title": "Everything else",
  "intro": "Black does not play 1...d5, or does not play it yet. One safe recipe each, and most of them come back to a position you already know.\n\n## The rule that covers nearly all of it\n\nBuild the same house. Nf3, e3, Bd3 or Be2, b3, Bb2, castle, knight to d2. There is no move order Black can choose that stops all seven of those, and most first moves that are not 1...d5 simply arrive at chapter 1 or chapter 4 a move or two later.\n\n## The two that need a decision\n\n**The Dutch, 1...f5.** Black takes e4 away and weakens the light squares to do it. Keep the bishop on d3, where it aims at h7 down a diagonal Black has just stopped defending with a knight, and put the other one on b2. Do not rush g4 or h3 and g4: the position is better for White by structure, not by force.\n\n**An early ...c5.** Meet it with 2.e3, hold the pawn on d4, and recapture towards the centre when Black takes. That gives White the half open e-file and a normal position, and it avoids the sharp gambit lines that follow 2.d5 or 2.Nf3 cxd4.\n\n## Transpositions\n\n1...Nf6 with a later ...d5 is chapter 1. 1...Nf6 with ...g6 is chapter 4. 1...e6 nearly always becomes chapter 1 as soon as Black plays ...d5. Notice the transposition early and you have already learnt the position.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nMeeting the Dutch with an immediate g4 or h3, which loses the structure that made White better. Taking on c5 without a reason and handing Black a free developing move. Playing Bd3 into a fianchetto out of habit. And treating a move order you have not seen before as new theory when it is a chapter you already know one move later.",
